---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import ForFramework from '@/components/ForFramework.astro';
import ApiName from '@/components/ApiName.astro';
import Choices from '@/components/motion/Choices.astro';
import Choice from '@/components/motion/Choice.astro';
import GoodToKnow from '@/components/motion/GoodToKnow.astro';
import Fact from '@/components/motion/Fact.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import { MotionGestureReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-gesture.tsx';
import { MotionGestureAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-gesture.ts';
import MotionGestureSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-gesture.svelte';
import { glossaryHref } from '@/lib/motion-glossary';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="gestures"
  title="Motion: drag, swipe, reorder"
  description="Drag, swipe and reorder with the pointer, each with the keyboard alternative it must have. In HTML, React, Angular and Svelte."
  frameworkSwitch
>
  <Fragment slot="summary">
    Drag, swipe or reorder an element with the pointer, each with the keyboard alternative it needs.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="when-to-use-it">When to use it</h2>
  <Choices size="compact">
    <Choice lead href="/motion/how-it-works#families" link="See the families in How it works">
      <Fragment slot="title"><ApiName html={'data-motion-gesture'} react="gesture" angular="udxMotionGesture" svelte="motionGesture()" /></Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="when">the visitor moves an element with the pointer: a queue to reorder, a card to swipe away.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="because">the keyboard alternative and the focus come with it, and a release settles on a spring.</Fragment>
    </Choice>
    <Choice code={false} title="Layout" when="Prefer it when" href="/motion/layout" link="Layout">
      <Fragment slot="when">the element moves because your state changed, not because the pointer moved it.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="because">the layout around the element carries it to its new place: the visitor has nothing to drag.</Fragment>
    </Choice>
    <Choice code={false} title="Values" when="Prefer it when" href="/motion/values" link="Values">
      <Fragment slot="when">a number follows the pointer and drives a style, such as a progress bar or a slider.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="because">the value is written to a CSS variable, and the element itself does not move.</Fragment>
    </Choice>
  </Choices>

  <h2 id="reorder-and-swipe-the-queue">Reorder and swipe the queue</h2>
  <p>
    The queue reorders with the pointer, or with Space and the arrow keys. The card under it is swiped away, and the button
    brings it back.
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">Set <code>data-motion-gesture</code> on the tracks and on the card, and call <code>connectMotion()</code> once: it binds each gesture.</p>
    <p slot="react">Pass <code>gesture</code> to a <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>, and move the track in your state from <code>onMotionReorder</code>.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Put <code>udxMotionGesture</code> on the element, and move the track in your list from <code>(udxReorder)</code>.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Attach <code>motionGesture()</code> to the element, and move the track in your array from <code>onReorder</code>.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-gesture" layout="split" previewHeight={392}>
    <MotionGestureReact slot="react" client:visible />
    <MotionGestureAngular slot="angular" client:visible />
    <MotionGestureSvelte slot="svelte" client:visible />
  </MotionUsage>

  <h2 id="how-it-behaves">How it behaves</h2>
  <h3 id="what-starts-it">What starts it</h3>
  <p>
    A <a href={glossaryHref('gesture')}>gesture</a> starts on a pointer press on the element, and so does the key of its
    keyboard alternative: Space or Enter for <code>grab</code>, Delete or Backspace for <code>action</code>, the arrow keys
    for <code>arrows</code>.
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">Name the gesture with <code>data-motion-gesture</code>. An element that cannot take focus gets <code>tabindex="0"</code>.</p>
    <p slot="react">Pass <code>gesture</code> to a <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>. An element that holds only a gesture needs no <code>name</code>.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Add <code>MotionGesture</code> to the component's <code>imports</code>, and put <code>udxMotionGesture</code> on the element.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Attach <code>motionGesture('complete')</code> to the element, with the name of its gesture.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    While the pointer holds the element, the runtime writes <code>data-gesture="dragging"</code> on it. A release is written
    as the element glides to rest on its spring.
  </p>

  <h3 id="what-each-type-does">What each type does</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>drag</code> follows the pointer on <code>axis</code> <code>x</code>, <code>y</code> or <code>both</code> (the
      default). It stays within the content box of its parent, or moves freely with <code>bounds: 'none'</code>. Released,
      it is thrown on by 0.2 s of its velocity, within its bounds. Its keyboard alternative is <code>arrows</code>: the arrow
      keys move it 16 px, or 64 px with Shift.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>swipe</code> follows the pointer on <code>axis</code> <code>x</code> (the default) or <code>y</code>. Released
      past 40 % of its size, or at more than 800 px/s in its direction, it flies out and fades; released sooner, it springs
      back. Its keyboard alternative is <code>action</code>: Delete or Backspace swipes the focused element towards the end.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>reorder</code> moves the element among its siblings that carry the same gesture, on <code>axis</code>
      <code>y</code> (the default) or <code>x</code>, each time its centre crosses a neighbour's. Its keyboard alternative is
      <code>grab</code>: Space or Enter grabs the focused element, the arrow keys of its axis move it, Space or Enter drops
      it, and Escape puts it back where it was.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="rules">Rules</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>An element carries one gesture at a time.</li>
    <li>
      While its gesture is bound, the element's <code>touch-action</code> leaves the browser only the pan that the gesture
      does not use: a touch screen moves the element, and still scrolls the page along the other axis.
    </li>
    <li>
      When a swiped element flies out with the focus inside it, the focus moves as the fly-out starts: to the next item with
      the same gesture, else the previous one, else the container.
    </li>
    <li>
      When the element leaves the page, its gesture is destroyed, and its previous <code>touch-action</code> comes back.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="variants">Variants</h2>
  <h3 id="dismiss-a-track-with-a-swipe">Dismiss a track with a swipe</h3>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">Set <code>afterFlyOut: 'remove'</code> in the definition: the swiped element leaves the DOM once it has flown out, and no script removes it.</p>
    <p slot="react">In <code>onMotionSwipe</code>, remove the track from your state. Keep <code>afterFlyOut</code> at its default, <code>hide</code>: the swiped element stays in the DOM, hidden from assistive technology, until React removes it.</p>
    <p slot="angular">In <code>(udxSwipe)</code>, remove the track from your list. Keep <code>afterFlyOut</code> at <code>hide</code>, so that Angular removes the element.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">In <code>onSwipe</code>, remove the track from your array. Keep <code>afterFlyOut</code> at <code>hide</code>, so that Svelte removes the element.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    You do not move the focus yourself. To send it elsewhere, name a target with
    <ApiName html={'data-motion-restore-focus="<selector>"'} react="restoreFocus" angular="[udxMotionRestoreFocus]" svelte="restoreFocus" />
    (an element, or a function that gives one then), or <code>false</code> to leave it where it is. A swipe you prevent keeps
    the element, and the focus with it.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-gestures-dismiss" live={false} />

  <h3 id="reorder-a-list">Reorder a list</h3>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">The element moves in the DOM as each step happens, so you need no code for the order. To keep the order somewhere else too, listen to <code>motion-reorder</code> on the list: <code>preventDefault()</code> there refuses the step.</p>
    <p slot="react">Pass <code>onMotionReorder</code>, and call <code>event.preventDefault()</code>. Then move the item in your state: React renders the new order, and the layout motion animates it.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Bind <code>(udxReorder)</code>, and call <code>$event.preventDefault()</code>. Then move the item in your state: the template renders the new order.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Pass <code>onReorder</code> to <code>motionGesture()</code>, and call <code>event.preventDefault()</code>. Then move the item in your state: Svelte renders the new order.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>A reorder announces each keyboard step in a visually hidden live region, for example <code>Moved. Position 3 of 3.</code></p>

  <h3 id="listen-to-what-the-gesture-reports">Listen to what the gesture reports</h3>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">The gesture dispatches DOM events on the element, and they bubble: <code>motion-dragend</code>, <code>motion-swipe</code> and <code>motion-reorder</code>. Read their <code>detail</code> in a listener.</p>
    <p slot="react">Pass <code>onMotionDragEnd</code>, <code>onMotionSwipe</code> or <code>onMotionReorder</code> to a <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>. Each receives the event, and its <code>detail</code>.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Bind <code>(udxDragEnd)</code>, <code>(udxSwipe)</code> or <code>(udxReorder)</code>. Each emits the event, and its <code>detail</code> is <code>$event.detail</code>.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Pass <code>onDragEnd</code>, <code>onSwipe</code> or <code>onReorder</code> to <code>motionGesture()</code>. Each receives the event, and its <code>detail</code>.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <ul>
    <li><code>drag</code> reports <code>detail: &#123; x, y &#125;</code> when a released drag has come to rest. It cannot be prevented.</li>
    <li>
      <code>swipe</code> reports <code>detail: &#123; direction &#125;</code>, <code>'start'</code> or <code>'end'</code>, when it is released far or
      fast enough, or when Delete or Backspace is pressed. It is cancelable: <code>preventDefault()</code> keeps the element
      in place, and it springs back.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>reorder</code> reports <code>detail: &#123; from, to &#125;</code> before each step, by pointer or by keyboard. It is
      cancelable: <code>preventDefault()</code> refuses the step.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="options">Options</h2>
  <p>A gesture takes a <code>type</code>, drag, swipe or reorder, and the options below.</p>
  <MotionOptions
    rows={[
      { name: 'type', values: 'drag · swipe · reorder', description: 'What the pointer does with the element. Required.' },
      { name: 'axis', values: 'x · y · both', default: 'both (drag) · x (swipe) · y (reorder)', description: 'The axis the element moves along. A drag takes x, y or both; a swipe and a reorder take x or y.' },
      { name: 'bounds', values: 'parent · none', default: 'parent', description: 'A drag stays within the content box of its parent, or moves freely. Drag only.' },
      { name: 'timing', values: 'spring.*', default: 'spring.default.spatial', description: 'The spring of the release, when the element comes to rest. A spring only: the release keeps the velocity of the pointer.' },
      { name: 'afterFlyOut', values: 'hide · remove', default: 'hide', description: 'What a swiped element becomes once it has flown out: kept in the DOM with aria-hidden, or removed from it. Swipe only.' },
      { name: 'alternative', values: 'arrows · action · grab', default: 'the one of the type', description: 'The keyboard alternative of the type: arrows for a drag, action for a swipe, grab for a reorder. It is inferred when you leave it out.' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2 id="good-to-know">Good to know</h2>
  <GoodToKnow>
    <Fact label="Reduced motion">
      <p>
        Under the reduced-motion <a href={glossaryHref('policy')}>policy</a>, or with <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on the
        element, a release is written at once, with no glide. The pointer still moves the element, and the keyboard
        alternatives are unchanged. See <a href="/motion/accessibility">Accessibility</a>.
      </p>
    </Fact>
    <Fact label="Timing">
      <p>
        A release comes to rest on the <a href={glossaryHref('spatial and effects springs')}>spatial</a> spring of its gesture:
        the default one, or the spring that the definition names with <code>timing</code>.
      </p>
      <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial']} />
    </Fact>
    <Fact label="Limits">
      <ul>
        <li>If the element needs its own <code>translate</code>, bind the gesture to an inner element: the gesture writes the <code>translate</code> of its element, and the <code>opacity</code> of a swipe while it flies out.</li>
        <li>If an item must not move in a reorder, leave it without the gesture: a reorder moves an element among the siblings that carry the same gesture, under the same parent.</li>
        <li>If a swiped element must leave the DOM, set <code>afterFlyOut: 'remove'</code>, as the HTML example does; otherwise, remove the element in your state, or it stays in the DOM with <code>aria-hidden</code>.</li>
        <li>If the spec of a gesture is wrong, the development build reports it, and the production build trusts it: check the page in development.</li>
        <ForFramework as="li">
          <Fragment slot="angular">If you render the page on the server, the gesture is not bound there: it is bound in the browser, once the element has rendered.</Fragment>
        </ForFramework>
        <li>Something else? See <a href="/motion/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a> and <a href="/motion/limits">Limits</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fact>
  </GoodToKnow>
</MotionPage>
